<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Must match the daemon's own $JOB (`com.${VISIONOCR_LABEL:-visionocr}.autonomous`) CHARACTER FOR
       CHARACTER: `park_run`, the RUN-STATUS-COMPLETE exit and `daemon.sh stop` all `launchctl bootout`
       this label. A label that does not match the one launchd registered means every intentional stop
       silently fails and KeepAlive relaunches the daemon it just tried to retire. -->
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.visionocr.autonomous</string>

  <!-- Runs the INSTALLED copy of the same long-lived loop (one argument, no wrapper). The script lives
       under ~/.local/bin, i.e. OUTSIDE ~/Desktop, and that placement is load-bearing rather than tidy: a
       launchd-context bash cannot exec anything under Desktop (TCC) — it dies with "Operation not
       permitted" and the job then silently no-ops on every relaunch. -->
  <!-- __HOME__ / __REPO__ are rendered by `daemon.sh` at install time. launchd expands NEITHER `~` NOR
       `$HOME` in a path, and the committed template has to stay user-agnostic, so the placeholders are the
       only way to have both. daemon.sh lints the RENDERED file, since that is what launchd actually loads. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>__HOME__/.local/bin/vision-ocr-autonomous.sh</string>
  </array>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <!-- The installed daemon sits outside any checkout, so it cannot derive the repo from its own location,
         and it REFUSES to start without this (there is deliberately no hardcoded fallback — guessing wrong
         means working in a directory that does not exist and failing obscurely every cycle). -->
    <key>VISIONOCR_REPO</key>
    <string>__REPO__</string>
    <!-- ⚠️ NOT boilerplate, and not something the daemon can recover from on its own. vision-ocr's CLAUDE.md
         lists it as an environment trap: "Backgrounded shell commands have essentially no PATH — basename,
         cut, timeout fail silently and loops report bogus results." A launchd job is the extreme case. The
         build needs /opt/homebrew/bin for `jbig2` and `qpdf`, and `build.sh` treats "not installed" as
         BENIGN — so a PATH-less launch would not fail, it would ship a bundle missing its own tools and
         report success. The daemon exports the same list itself; this is the belt to that braces, because
         launchd's environment is what everything before that line inherits. -->
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- KeepAlive=true: the only thing that STOPS this daemon is a `launchctl bootout`, which every
       INTENTIONAL stop performs (park_run, RUN STATUS: COMPLETE, `daemon.sh stop`). Any OTHER death — a
       crash, an OOM kill, a stray SIGTERM — leaves the job registered, so launchd relaunches it.
       ⚠️ DELIBERATELY NOT {SuccessfulExit: false}: the daemon traps SIGTERM/INT/HUP and exits 0, so a
       stray TERM would look "successful" and would NOT be restarted. That is exactly how a real daemon
       death went unnoticed in the sibling project (2026-07-17: TERMed mid-session, nothing restarted it,
       and the only evidence was a "daemon up" line with no matching "daemon down"). KeepAlive=true makes
       any bootout-less death restart, closing that gap. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Don't hammer on a startup crash-loop: 60s minimum between (re)launches. Also the reason
       `daemon.sh`'s post-launch poll can legitimately come up empty — see step 7 there. -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>60</integer>

  <!-- launchd's own view of the job, separate from the daemon's $STATE/daemon.log: these two files are
       where a failure BEFORE the daemon's first log line lands (a bad interpreter, a TCC refusal, an
       unset VISIONOCR_REPO). Read them first when `daemon up` never appears. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/.local/state/visionocr-autonomous/launchd.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/.local/state/visionocr-autonomous/launchd.err.log</string>

  <!-- A LaunchAgent loads in the GUI login session. So this survives a daemon CRASH but NOT a logout or a
       reboot, and reboot-survival is deliberately out of scope: bringing the run back after a reboot means
       a LaunchDaemon in a root domain with no logged-in user's keychain, TCC grants or `caffeinate`
       assertion — none of which this daemon can work without. -->
  <!-- ⛔ WAS `Background` UNTIL 2026-08-24, AND THAT COST 5.14x ON EVERY SUITE RUN. Measured end to
       end, same commit `8d00504`, same 1,247 checks, same machine still holding 4.0 GB of swap: 709 s
       in the normal band against a mean of 3,643 s over the daemon's four runs (3,362 / 3,610 / 3,617
       / 3,982). `Background` is darwin-bg — lowest QoS, I/O throttled, threads restricted to the
       E-cores — and per `man taskpolicy` ("All children of the specified program also inherit these
       policies") ALL CHILDREN INHERIT IT, so the clamp reached launchd -> daemon loop -> claude
       session -> git commit -> pre-commit hook -> run_tests.sh -> build/tests. It also SURVIVES THE
       PARENT'S DEATH: a detached mutation campaign stayed clamped after the daemon was booted out.
       Two microbenchmarks put the band's cost at 5.4x and 6.6x, which BRACKET the 5.14x end-to-end
       figure rather than confirm it.
       ⛔ AND IT CANNOT BE ESCAPED FROM INSIDE, so do not "fix" this in `test-lock.sh` instead —
       measured 2026-08-24: `taskpolicy -B -p $$` inside a darwin-bg process leaves PRI=4, leaves its
       children at PRI=4, and does not improve the benchmark (1.811 -> 1.991 s, i.e. no escape; that
       spread is run-to-run noise on a contended machine). `-B` works only from OUTSIDE, on another
       pid. This plist is the only lever.
       ⚠️ `PRI` IS THE DISCRIMINATOR, NOT `nice`: penalized is PRI=4, normal is PRI=31+, and NI=5 on
       its own costs ~3%. Reading NI as evidence of the band cost one killed measurement that day.
       ⚠️ WHY `Standard`: documented as "equivalent to no ProcessType being set", which the same man
       page says still applies LIGHT CPU/IO throttling — that is the intended deference, and it is why
       no `Nice` key is set. A `Nice 5` was drafted and dropped: its only measurement was on an IDLE
       machine (~3%), where nice by definition costs nothing, and the verify step below cannot tell a
       honoured `Nice` from a silently ignored one. `Interactive` means "no resource limitations" and
       is for work a user waits on. NOT `Adaptive`: it moves between Background and Interactive on XPC
       transaction activity, and this daemon opens none, so it would sit in Background — the bug again.
       ⚠️ INFERENCE, NOT MEASUREMENT: the 709 s run came from a shell in the normal band, not from
       launchd under `ProcessType Standard`. That `Standard` lands in that same band is the assumption
       this change rests on, which is what the check below is for.
       ⚠️ VERIFY AFTER THE NEXT `start` — `-O` landed in `run_tests.sh` in the same change, so the
       target is BOTH fixes together: the first `pre-commit` row in `$STATE/suite-timings.tsv` should
       be ~225 s. ~700 s means the band fix landed and `-O` did not; ~3,600 s means neither did, and
       `Interactive` is the fallback for a `Standard` that throttles more than "lightly".
       ⚠️ AND THIS ONLY REACHES THE `keepalive` LANE: `daemon.sh`'s `nohup` lane never touches this
       plist, so a daemon started that way inherits the launching shell's band instead.
       ⛔ THE TIMEOUT CONSTANTS WERE ALL DERIVED FROM CLAMPED RUNS — `MAXRUN`/`GATE_MAXRUN` (14400),
       `HB_HARD` (3600), `test-lock.sh`'s `WAIT_DEFAULT`/`MAXAGE`, and the hook's own duplicated
       `hook_wait`. DO NOT RESCALE THEM BY ANY RATIO ON THIS PAGE: the mean-of-four above is exactly
       the method `vision-ocr-autonomous.sh:193` and `test-lock.sh:65` forbid in capitals — "the WORST
       row in suite-timings.tsv you are willing to survive, plus headroom; never the mean, never one
       run" — two overruns PARK the run, and `GATE_MAXRUN` has already been under-sized twice. None of
       them is purely suite-bound either: `GATE_MAXRUN` carries a 3,600 s lock-wait term this change
       does not shrink, and `MAXRUN` bounds a whole session dominated by model latency. Re-derive from
       the worst NEW row once enough have accumulated, or leave them oversized — oversized is safe.
       ⚠️ CONCURRENCY, CORRECTED: the suite ran at ~95% of ONE core in the run measured here, but do
       not generalise that to "it can only ever take one core" — the batch path is an `OperationQueue`
       at `Sources/Model.swift:1292` whose `maxConcurrentOperationCount` is `Prefs.defaultConcurrency`
       = min(perflevel0.logicalcpu, 8) = 6 on this M3 Pro, with Vision threaded on top. -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Standard</string>
</dict>
</plist>
